<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Rainforest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures.<br>Communication: Relay simple information received verbally using English or Spanish.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures (e.g., draw a slow sloth to show the story's main idea).<br>Can relay simple information received verbally by pointing to images (e.g., points to "furry animal" on a picture of a sloth).<br>Can describe new vocabulary in a story using drawing and pictures (e.g., describing the rainforest showing a picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher shows large flashcards of rainforest animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, frog). Students identify them by name (e.g., "It's a sloth!"). Teacher asks "What animal is it?" for each card. Students point and say the name.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher holds up a picture of a sloth and says, "This is a sloth. The sloth is slow and furry." Teacher then takes a blank paper and quickly draws a simple representation of a slow, furry animal, saying, "I can draw a slow sloth." This models how to summarize information (slow, furry) with a drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the sloth fast or slow?" (Students say "Slow!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the drawing of the sloth) "What animal is this?" (Students say "A sloth!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture book or digital presentation of "A Day in the Rainforest" with simple sentences describing animals. Example text: "This is the rainforest. Many animals live here. Look! This is a toucan. It is colorful and can fly. This is a monkey. It is jumpy and likes to climb vines."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Children understand simple descriptions of rainforest animals and their actions from a short narrative. They identify key information to be summarized visually or verbally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Teacher asks, "What place is this story about?" (Students point to "rainforest" picture or say "rainforest").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Teacher shows two flashcards (e.g., "colorful toucan," "jumpy monkey") and asks, "Which animal is colorful?" Students point to the toucan. "Which animal likes to climb?" Students point to the monkey.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful toucan, furry monkey). WH-questions ("What animal is it?"). Present simple ("It's the rainforest."). Vocabulary: rainforest, sloth, toucan, monkey, colorful, slow, furry, jumpy, climb, fly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen, Draw, and Match (Controlled Practice):</b> Teacher reads simple sentences about an animal (e.g., "It's a bird. It's colorful. It flies in the rainforest."). Students listen and draw the animal. Then, they match their drawing to a picture flashcard of the toucan. Teacher reinforces /r/ in "rainforest" and "colorful."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Building with Pictures (Semi-controlled):</b> Teacher provides sentence frames like "This is a _____. It is _____ and _____." Students choose animal pictures (e.g., sloth, monkey) and adjective cards (e.g., slow, furry, jumpy, noisy) to complete the sentences verbally, pointing to the corresponding visuals. (e.g., "This is a monkey. It is jumpy and noisy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Tell Your Partner" (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student gets one picture of a rainforest animal. They show their picture to their partner and relay one simple fact or description using English or Spanish (e.g., "This is a sloth. It is slow."). The partner listens and repeats or points to the corresponding word if available. Teacher encourages /s/ in "sloth."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual "Rainforest Animal Profile" Creation and Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work on the "Rainforest Animal Profiles" project. Each student receives a blank "Animal Profile Card" (a large index card or half-sheet of paper) with prompts like "Animal Name:" and space for a drawing. Teacher provides simplified "research" – very short sentences about two or three rainforest animals (e.g., "The sloth sleeps a lot. It moves very slow. It has furry skin.") written on large charts with matching pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one animal from the provided research (e.g., sloth).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw that animal on their profile card, incorporating key visual traits (e.g., slow movement for a sloth, colorful feathers for a toucan). This is their visual summary of the information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, students practice relaying simple information about their chosen animal to a peer. They point to their drawing and say 1-2 sentences using target vocabulary (e.g., "This is a slow sloth." or "The toucan is colorful."). If they struggle with English, they can use Spanish for one word and the teacher or peer helps translate or model. This acts as mediation practice for their project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering assistance with vocabulary (e.g., "furry," "leafy") and pronunciation (/s/, /r/, /dʒ/).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback (Thumbs Up/Down).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student briefly presents their "Rainforest Animal Profile" to a small group or the teacher. They show their drawing and verbally relay one or two simple pieces of information about the animal using English, pointing to their drawing as they speak. (e.g., "This is a sloth. It is slow.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation:</b> Can summarize key traits using drawings/pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communication:</b> Can relay simple information (1-2 sentences) about the animal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least one target adjective (e.g., slow, colorful, furry).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Overall intelligibility, particularly /s/ in "sloth" and /r/ in "rainforest."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Listens attentively when peers present.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up their animal profile and answer:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I draw my animal? (Yes/No)" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say one thing about my animal in English?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I like about making my animal profile?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word I learned about the rainforest?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students summarize and relay information using drawings? (e.g., clarity of the simplified research, student engagement with drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better describe animals or use more English in their relaying? (e.g., providing sentence starters on the profile card, more explicit modeling of using pictures to explain).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> As this is the final lesson of the "It's the Rainforest" theme, students have completed the first iteration of their "Rainforest Animal Profiles" project. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, building on the descriptive language and project-based learning skills developed here, potentially creating profiles for a different habitat or type of animal.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>